Ladybird now decides whether a line can break before an atomic inline
by the white-space of the nearest common ancestor of the box and the
content before it, for line breaking and for min-content sizing alike.
Blink does the same: Its LineBreaker takes auto_wrap_ from the inline
box it’s in, so an atomic inline breaks by its parent’s style, and
LineBreaker::CanBreakAfterAtomicInline() checks that one. WebKit’s
InlineFormattingUtils::isAtSoftWrapOpportunity() uses the style of
nearestCommonAncestor() for a boundary next to an atomic inline, and
Gecko’s nsLineLayout::ReflowFrame() records the break after one only
where its span’s mNoWrap allows it.

Ladybird lets a line break between a word joiner or a no-break space
and an atomic inline after it, though css-text-3 rules that break
out: Chrome 158.0.8127.37, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5 all break
there in layout. For min-content, Firefox and WebKit break there too,
and Chrome doesn’t. Checked in Chrome 158.0.8127.37, Firefox 151.0,
and WebKit 26.5, with Ahem.
